{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If a man vows a vow</b> — one who says: 'It is incumbent upon me [as] a konam that I not eat' or 'that I not do such-and-such a thing.' Could it be even if he said that he should eat nevelah, do I call upon him 'according to all that proceeds from his mouth he shall do'? Scripture teaches 'to bind a binding' — to bind the permitted and not to permit the forbidden. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 30:3). But the language of Sifrei (Matot 153) is not so; rather, they taught regarding 'to bind a binding': Could it be even if he swore to eat nevelot and terefot, shekatzim and remasim, do I call upon him 'according to all that proceeds from his mouth he shall do'? Scripture teaches 'to bind a binding upon himself' — to bind the permitted and not to permit the forbidden. They mentioned this matter in [the tractate of] Shevuot, and the Rav substituted Nedarim for it. In my opinion he was not careful, for they said (Nedarim 2a) regarding vows that [the formula] creates a forbidden object upon him, and therefore they do not take effect upon something that has no substance, such as 'a vow upon me that I not speak with you' or 'that I not go' or 'that I not sleep' and similar things. And since this is so, it appears that even regarding a matter of reshut, if he said 'a vow upon me that I eat today' or 'that I eat this loaf' — it is not a vow, for the vow is not upon the object at all but upon himself that he do it, and vows are not mentioned in the Gemara in the category of 'arise and do' at all. And although we find in vows to Heaven 'an olah is incumbent upon me,' 'shelamim are incumbent upon me,' whose declaration to Heaven obligates his property in it like his delivery to a private person — or because there is a stringency practiced in vows of hekdesh since there is an object that becomes forbidden to the private person and is seized by Heaven when he sets it aside, and therefore from the first moment the obligation of the vow takes effect upon his property — but in vows of expression we do not have [this], because there is no object there at all, neither at the beginning nor at the end. And likewise 'to bind the permitted and not to permit the forbidden' — it is not fitting to mention it regarding vows, because they take effect upon a matter of mitzvah just as upon a matter of reshut, and one who says in a vow 'a sukkah that I will not make,' 'tefillin that I will not don' — is forbidden (ibid. 16a), even though he transgresses a positive commandment. And likewise one who forbids his wife from marital relations is forbidden (ibid. 14b), even though he nullifies a negative commandment. And likewise they take effect in the fulfillment of a negative commandment, such as one who vowed not to eat nevelah, as we learned (ibid. 17a ff.) 'there is a vow within a vow' etc. It turns out that the vow takes effect to bind the forbidden and to permit for himself that he not do what it is forbidden to transgress — except that they do not take effect to transgress with hands a negative commandment. But oaths are not [so] at all — only to bind the permitted; they do not apply to a matter of mitzvah at all, neither in nullifying a negative commandment nor in fulfilling it, nor in nullifying a positive commandment, such as 'an oath that I will not make a sukkah' and 'that I will not don tefillin.' And even in the fulfillment of a positive commandment they do not take effect, for if he swore to fulfill the mitzvah and did not fulfill it, he is not liable for it on account of oath, neither lashes nor an offering, but it is merely permitted to him to spur himself on (ibid. 8a), as it is written 'I have sworn and I will fulfill it, to observe Your righteous ordinances' (Psalms 119:106). And therefore in oaths alone is 'to bind a binding upon himself' required. And these are great halakhot; the discerning one will find them in their places." "And the reason for <b>shall vow a vow to YHWH</b> is not like '[who] swore to YHWH' (Psalms 132:2), for that is like 'and he swore to him' (Genesis 24:9), that he swore to him for his need. But in an oath one says 'and by His name you shall swear' (Deuteronomy 6:13), 'and I will make you swear by YHWH' (Genesis 24:3), 'as I swore to you by YHWH, the Elohim of Israel, saying: Solomon your son shall reign after me' (II Kings 1:30), 'Swear to me here by Elohim' (Genesis 21:23). But in a vow one does not say 'he vows by YHWH' but 'to YHWH.' And the Sages already hinted at the reason for the matter in Sifrei (Matot 14); they said: What is the difference between vows and oaths? In vows it is like vowing by the life of the king; in oaths it is like swearing by the king himself. Although there is no proof for the matter, it is a remembrance of the matter: 'As YHWH lives and as your soul lives, I will not leave you' (II Kings 4:30). And the secret is that the oath [shevu'ah] is from the language of seven [sheva], for she has built her house, she has hewn its pillars seven, and the vow is — in understanding the beginning of his way, the first of his works of old. It is found that vows ascend upon [the foundation of] Torah, and therefore they take effect upon a matter of mitzvah as upon a matter of reshut, and behold every vow is to YHWH and every oath is in Him."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The word קונם (konam/qonam) is left transliterated as it is a technical formula-term in the rabbinic law of vows, derived from קונם but functioning as a quasi-sacred substitution. The parenthetical Talmud references (e.g., 'ibid. 16a') resolve abbreviations like (שם טז) where שם refers back to Nedarim.", 1 "The phrases 'she has built her house, she has hewn its pillars seven' and 'in understanding the beginning of his way, the first of his works of old' are adapted from Proverbs 9:1 and Proverbs 8:22–23 respectively; Ramban weaves them together as kabbalistic allusions and they are rendered here hyper-literally without expansion. The final line 'every vow is to YHWH and every oath is in Him' echoes the distinction drawn above: vows use ל ('to') and oaths use ב ('by/in')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:01.002102216Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}